Thanks for the update. I measured 333af0f against its 6.4 base (Console suite green on PHP 8.5 and on 8.1 with symfony/string 6.4; the 6 behaviour tests fail on the base as expected).

The wrapper change holds up: 1KB of CJK wraps in 36µs instead of 507µs here, and texts containing U+FDD0 wrap exactly as on 6.4. Text made of distinct characters gets no benefit (10KB of distinct ideographs: 51µs on 6.4, 5.0ms now), which looks acceptable for console messages.

`Helper::substrToWidth()` is still quadratic though: it drops one character per iteration and measures the whole prefix each time, and `FormatterHelper::truncate()` accepts any length.

| `truncate()` on 10KB of CJK | 6.4 | this PR | binary search |
|---|---|---|---|
| length 120 | 2.8ms | 8.3ms | 3.3ms |
| length 2000 | 2.8ms | 1111ms | 13ms |

The width only grows with the length, so a binary search returns the same result. It gave identical output on 36,001 random cases (ASCII, CJK, emoji, ZWJ sequences, combining marks, control characters, newlines, U+FDD0, widths from -7 to 100), and the Console suite passes with it:

```php
    public static function substrToWidth(?string $string, int $width): string
    {
        $substring = self::substr($string, 0, $width);

        if ($width <= 0 || self::width($substring) <= $width) {
            return $substring;
        }

        // the width grows with the length: search the longest beginning that fits
        $min = 0;
        $max = $width - 1;
        while ($min < $max) {
            $length = ($min + $max + 1) >> 1;
            if (self::width(self::substr($string, 0, $length)) <= $width) {
                $min = $length;
            } else {
                $max = $length - 1;
            }
        }

        return self::substr($string, 0, $min);
    }
```
